
=======================================================================
Overview current design:

   - The Pscf::SweepTmpl class template takes the typename of a data 
     structure that stores a "State" (fields and possibly unit cell 
     parameters) as a template parameter. 

   - The Pscf::SweepTmpl template does not take the System type as a 
     template parameer, and so cannot define code for any operation 
     that involves communication with the System, and cannot pass 
     the system as a parameter to any functions.

   - The Sweep class in each program-level namespace is derived from 
     a specialization of Pscf::SweepTmpl with a specific choice of 
     data type for a State.

=======================================================================
Critique of current design:

   - This design forces the use of the same State class by all
     subclasses of Sweep in any program-level namespace.
     This makes it impossible to create different Sweep classes for 
     periodic systems that are designed for use with, respectively, 
     symmetry-preserving iterators, for which states are stored in 
     symmetry-adapted basis format, or a non-symmetry preserving 
     iterators, for which states should be stored using values on 
     a grid.

   - This pattern encourages coding of implementation details that
     depend on the choice of State class into the program-level Sweep 
     class, since is the highest level class that "knows" about the 
     choice of a specific State class type. 
 
   - Each program-level Sweep class should only define a public 
     interface and perhaps some protected members for data and
     operations that are expected to be the same for all types of 
     Sweep subclasses. It should not specify a type for the state
     class.

    - A specialization of the SweepTmpl class (which provide a 
     skeleton implementation for a specific type of state) should 
     be a subclass of the program-level "Sweep" class, rather than 
     the reverse.

   - The Pscf::SweepTmpl class now provides code for two logically 
     separate purposes:

     (1) Use of Lagrange polynomial exptrapolation to create an 
     extrapolated initial conditions for each state in a sweep.

     (2) Maintenance of a list of specialized ParameterType objects
     for parameters that are not part of the standard list. 

     There is no overlap in data structures or functions used for
     these two purposes. In retrospect, it seems clear that should
     be implemented in separate classes, and perhaps should be used 
     by composition rather than inheritance.

   - The SweepTmpl template cannot provide the code to extrapolate
     states because there is no standardized interface for the
     linear algebra operations required to construct linear 
     superpositions of states.

